  "lineage": "Mexican narrative-ballad song-form documented mid-19th-century onward, descended from Spanish-romance medieval narrative-poetic-tradition and re-rooted in the Mexican-revolutionary-era (1910-1920) as the canonical journalistic-historical-record song-form of Mexican rural-popular consciousness. The canonical corrido structure: 4-line octosyllabic-rhyming verses, typically 8-15 verses long, narrating a single historical-event (battles, bandit-exploits, assassinations, romantic-tragedies, social-injustice). Performed solo (guitar-and-voice) or with mariachi-conjunto, the corrido emphasizes textual-narrative-clarity over musical-elaboration (simple I-IV-V harmonic-progression, often unchanged across all verses). The modern narco-corrido subgenre 1970s-onward chronicles drug-trafficking-figures, replacing the revolutionary-era revolutionary-hero subject-matter with contemporary cartel-leadership.",
